{"meta":{"chapter_title":"AI Ethics and Ethobot 3.2","title":"Privacy and Consent With Minors","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting privacy and consent with minors to school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"04","section":"04","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_ethics_and_ethobot_3_2","cat531_supplement_privacy_and_consent_with_minors","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/04/03"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_4_4_privacy_and_consent_with_minors_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_4_4_privacy_and_consent_with_minors_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_4_4_privacy_and_consent_with_minors_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_4_4_privacy_and_consent_with_minors_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":4,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Privacy and Consent With Minors\n\n![Privacy and Consent With Minors textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/04-04-privacy-and-consent-with-minors.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nConsent is the one place where good intentions are not enough, because minors cannot legally give it. A teacher who says \"I told the students the chatbot would save their questions\" has not obtained consent; she has issued a notice to people who lack the legal capacity to agree. The gap between notifying, assenting, and consenting is where most well-meaning classroom AI runs aground, and closing that gap is the core competency of this section. Privacy law treats children differently for a reason, and a defensible policy memo has to honor that difference in its design, not just its tone.\n\nThe stakes are higher with AI tools specifically because their default business model often runs against the student. Many vendors retain student inputs to train their models, sometimes for years, by default. A teacher who adopts the tool without changing that default has, in effect, enrolled minors in a data pipeline their families never agreed to. The consent clause you learn to write here is the mechanism that flips the default back toward the student.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Distinguish notification, assent, and consent, and assign each to the correct party (student assents; parent or guardian consents).\n- Specify what data an AI tool collects, the single purpose it is collected for, and what it is explicitly not used for.\n- Choose opt-in with parental consent over opt-out, and explain why a minor family's silence is not agreement.\n- Write a retention and deletion rule that overrides the vendor's default, and cite the vendor setting that made the clause necessary.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA teacher wants to use an AI homework chatbot with her 6th-grade class. The tool stores each student's questions to \"personalize\" help, and the vendor's default retains those logs for 24 months and uses them to train the model. The teacher's instinct is to send a friendly note home: \"Your child will be using a helpful AI tutor this term.\" That note feels responsible but does almost nothing legally or ethically. It does not name what data is collected, it does not ask for agreement, and it treats a non-response as a yes. This section is about turning that note into a consent clause that would survive a privacy officer's review.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nPrivacy and consent with minors rest on three terms that are routinely blurred and must be kept separate:\n\n| Term | Who acts | What it means | Legally sufficient alone? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Notification | Teacher tells | \"Here is what will happen.\" | No |\n| Assent | Minor agrees | The student's age-appropriate \"yes.\" | No |\n| Consent | Parent or guardian agrees | Legally binding permission for a minor. | Yes, when informed |\n\nA defensible consent design does four things in order. It names both parties (the student assents, the guardian consents). It states the data and a single bounded purpose (\"question text and timestamps, used only to alert the teacher to a struggling student, never to train the vendor model\"). It sets the default to opt-in, because a family that does not return a form has not agreed to anything. And it sets a retention and deletion rule that overrides the vendor's, with a specific end date.\n\nThe course anchors are concrete here. **AI and education datafication** is the lens that exposes the vendor default as the real adversary: the 24-month retention and training-on-student-data setting is the thing your clause exists to override. **Critical edtech studies** reminds you that the families least able to parse a dense consent form are often the ones whose children are most exposed, so plain language is an equity requirement, not a courtesy. **Teacher professional judgment** is what bounds the purpose: you decide the data serves a teacher alert and nothing more, and you write that limit in.\n\n## Worked Example: A Consent Clause for a Minors' AI Tool\n\nA teacher drafts the consent section of a policy memo for a 6th-grade class chatbot. Minors cannot give legal consent, so the design of the consent step is a judgment call. Work it in numbered steps.\n\nThe tool: an AI homework chatbot that stores each student's questions to \"personalize\" help.\n\nStep-by-step consent decision:\n\n- Step 1. Separate assent from consent. Students (minors) can assent; the parent or guardian gives legal consent. The memo must name both.\n- Step 2. State what data is collected and why. Question text and timestamps, used only to surface a struggling-student alert to the teacher, not to train the vendor model.\n- Step 3. Set the default. Default to opt-in with parental consent, not opt-out, because silence from a minor's family is not consent.\n- Step 4. Name the retention and deletion rule. Delete chat logs at the end of the grading term; do not let the vendor retain them.\n\nBefore (weak): \"Parents will be notified that students use the chatbot.\" Notification is not consent, and it omits data use and retention.\n\nAfter (strong): \"Consent clause: parental opt-in required before any student account is created. Collected data = question text and timestamps, used only for a teacher struggling-student alert, never for vendor model training. Logs deleted at term end. Students give assent on a plain-language screen. Evidence the clause is needed = the vendor's default setting retains logs for 24 months and trains on them.\"\n\nThe strong clause separates assent from consent, names data use and retention, defaults to opt-in, and points to the vendor setting that made the clause necessary.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Build the consent clause step by step\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The chatbot stores 6th-graders’ questions. Before you draft anything, who can give legally binding consent for a minor?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"The student, with an age-appropriate yes\",\"The parent or guardian; the student only assents\",\"The teacher, on the family’s behalf\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Parents or guardians consent; minors can only assent. The memo must name both parties as separate steps, because a minor’s yes is not legally sufficient.\"},{\"prompt\":\"You state the data collected. Which purpose statement is strong enough to protect students?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"\\\"Used to improve learning\\\"\",\"\\\"Question text and timestamps, used only for a teacher struggling-student alert, never to train the vendor model\\\"\",\"\\\"Used as the vendor sees fit\\\"\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Purpose limitation only protects students if it is narrow enough to exclude the vendor’s training pipeline by name. A vague purpose quietly licenses almost any data use.\"},{\"prompt\":\"You set the default. Opt-in or opt-out?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Opt-out: assume yes unless a family objects\",\"Opt-in: no form returned means no account created\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Opt-in. Silence from a minor’s family is not agreement, and opt-out inverts the protection minors are owed.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Finally, set a deletion rule that overrides the vendor’s 24-month training default: delete chat logs at term end, and cite that vendor setting as the reason the clause exists. Now the clause names both parties, bounds the purpose, defaults to opt-in, and overrides retention with cited evidence.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore any minor uses an AI tool that records them, the consent design must satisfy four tests:\n\n1. **Capacity:** Is legal consent sought from a guardian, with student assent as a separate, age-appropriate step?\n2. **Purpose limitation:** Is the data tied to one named use and explicitly walled off from model training?\n3. **Default:** Is the tool opt-in, so that no form returned means no account created?\n4. **Retention:** Is there a deletion date that overrides the vendor default, with the vendor setting cited?\n\nIf any test fails, the tool is not ready, regardless of how useful it is.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A teacher sends home: 'Your child will be using a helpful AI tutor this term.' The tool retains questions for 24 months and trains on them. What has the teacher actually obtained?\" explanation=\"A notification, not consent. The note names no data, asks for no agreement, treats silence as a yes, and never overrides the vendor''s retention-and-training default. Notification is legally insufficient for minors.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Valid consent, because families were informed\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Only a notification; no consent, no purpose limit, no opt-in, no retention override\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Student assent, which is sufficient for a minor\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"A binding agreement once the term begins\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nThe harder judgment is recognizing when no consent design rescues a tool. Walk a webcam-monitoring proposal through the four tests and decide.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The webcam behavior-tracking decision\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"A vendor offers an AI app that logs which students are off task via continuous webcam. The principal likes the classroom-management data. You apply the capacity test first. What does webcam observation of minors require?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A posted notice in the classroom is enough\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"A posted notice is notification, not consent. Webcam observation of minors plainly requires guardian consent.\"},{\"label\":\"Guardian consent, not just a notice\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Continuous observation of minors clears no lower bar than explicit guardian consent.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"Reconsider: is telling families the same as asking them?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"No; minors require guardian consent, not notification\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Move to the purpose test.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"Purpose-limitation test. The vendor wants to feed off-task logs into a behavior analytics dashboard. What is the narrowest defensible purpose?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Any classroom-management or disciplinary use the school finds helpful\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Too broad. A vague purpose licenses disciplinary records and vendor analytics, the exact uses purpose-limitation must exclude.\"},{\"label\":\"One use only: the teacher’s own reflection, barred from disciplinary records and vendor analytics\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"This is the narrow bound. But keep going; the retention test may still sink the tool.\"}]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"Tighten the purpose to exclude the training and disciplinary pipelines by name.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Limit to the teacher’s own reflection only\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Now the purpose is narrow enough to evaluate honestly.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"n5\":{\"prompt\":\"Retention test. The vendor’s default cloud storage keeps footage 90 days. Standing video of children is a severe risk. What is your decision now that you have run all four tests?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Adopt with same-day deletion and guardian opt-in\",\"next\":\"n6\",\"feedback\":\"Same-day deletion helps, but step back: continuous webcam monitoring of minors fails the purpose-and-proportion test no matter how the clause is worded.\"},{\"label\":\"Decline: no consent design rescues continuous webcam monitoring of minors\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"This is the competency the section names: recognizing when the honest answer is decline, because the tool fails proportionality even with a perfect clause.\"}]},\"n6\":{\"prompt\":\"Re-examine proportionality: does any clause make continuous video of children proportionate to classroom-management value?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"No; the honest answer is to decline the tool\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"Right. A good consent clause cannot save a disproportionate tool.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"end\":{\"prompt\":\"You declined the webcam tool on proportionality grounds.\",\"feedback\":\"The four tests sometimes conclude that no consent design is adequate. Recognizing when to decline is itself the judgment.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common error is mistaking notification for consent: telling families is not asking them, and assuming a yes from silence inverts the protection minors are owed. A subtler error is naming a vague purpose (\"to improve learning\") that quietly licenses almost any data use. Purpose limitation only protects students if the purpose is narrow enough to exclude the vendor's training pipeline by name.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the four tests to a different tool: an AI behavior-tracking app that logs which students are \"off task\" via webcam. Capacity: webcam observation of minors plainly requires guardian consent, not just a posted notice. Purpose limitation: the data must be tied to one use (a teacher's own classroom-management reflection) and explicitly barred from any disciplinary record or vendor analytics. Default: opt-in, with a non-camera alternative for families who decline, so opting out carries no penalty. Retention: footage deleted same-day, since standing video of children is a severe risk and the vendor's default cloud storage runs 90 days. Once you walk the tests, the honest conclusion may be that no consent design rescues this tool, because continuous webcam monitoring of minors fails the purpose-and-proportion test no matter how the clause is worded. Recognizing when the answer is \"decline\" is itself the competency.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nKeep a two-column note: each of the four tests on the left, the exact clause language that satisfies it on the right, with the vendor default it overrides in the margin. A strong reader can point to the line that turns the vendor's 24-month training default off, not just assert that privacy is protected. If your note says \"we got consent\" without naming who consented, for what bounded purpose, and against which vendor setting, the consent is decorative.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong policy memo separates assent from consent, states a bounded purpose, defaults to opt-in, sets a deletion date, and cites the vendor setting each clause overrides. Polish is the last layer. If the memo names a vague purpose or omits the retention override, mark the gap and ask BigAL to show the vendor default your clause needs to counter rather than a general explanation of privacy.\n\nA useful closing line for the artifact sounds like this: \"I required parental opt-in and walled the data off from vendor training because the tool's default retains and trains on student questions for 24 months; the clause deletes logs at term end, which the vendor setting otherwise would not.\"\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Explain why opt-in with parental consent is required rather than opt-out, using the idea that a minor family''s silence is not agreement.\" answer=\"Opt-out treats a non-response as a yes, which means a family that never saw the form, could not read it, or simply did not return it is enrolled by default. For adults that inversion is already questionable; for minors it is worse, because the protection the law gives children exists precisely to stop their data being used without an affirmative decision from a guardian. Opt-in forces that affirmative decision: no form returned means no account created, so the burden of action falls on getting agreement rather than on a family to prevent something they may never have understood was happening. Silence is the absence of consent, not its presence.\"></self-explain>\n\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the consent design to an 18-year-old dual-enrollment class, where students are legal adults. The capacity test changes: students now consent for themselves, and a separate guardian step is no longer required. But the purpose-limitation, opt-in, and retention tests do not relax at all, and treating adult students as if consent is automatic is its own error. Working this version tests whether you understand consent as a structured set of protections or merely as \"ask the parents.\"\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/04-04-privacy-and-consent-with-minors-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"04.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **TPACK**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks for CAT 531 04.04. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Concept Map\n\nThese are the concepts this section connects. Trace how separating consent from notification turns a vendor default into an evidence-based clause.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_ai_ethics_and_ethobot_3_2\", \"cat531_supplement_privacy_and_consent_with_minors\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_privacy_and_consent_with_minors\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks captures instructional purpose, equity or privacy boundary, support request, revision rationale, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- TPACK. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- AI and education datafication. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_4_privacy_and_consent_with_minors_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_privacy_and_consent_with_minors","stem":"A policy memo states: 'Parents will be notified that their child uses the AI chatbot.' Why is this insufficient as a consent provision for minors?","options":["It should be written in a larger font.","Notification is not consent; collecting a minor's data requires active parental opt-in, not just a notice.","It does not name the chatbot's vendor.","It uses the word child instead of student."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Informing parents is weaker than obtaining consent; minors' data collection needs active opt-in.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_4_notification_is_consent","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_privacy_and_consent_with_minors","evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_4_privacy_and_consent_with_minors_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_privacy_and_consent_with_minors","stem":"A 6th grader clicks 'I agree' on a chatbot's terms screen. What does this action legally provide, and what is still required?","options":["It provides full legal consent, so nothing else is required.","It provides student assent; legal consent from a parent or guardian is still required for a minor.","It provides parental consent by proxy.","It voids the school's responsibility for the data."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Minors give assent; the legally required consent comes from the parent or guardian.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_4_minor_can_consent","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_privacy_and_consent_with_minors","evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_4_privacy_and_consent_with_minors_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_privacy_and_consent_with_minors","stem":"A homework chatbot stores students' question text to 'personalize help.' Which question must the memo answer before approving data collection?","options":["Which color theme the chatbot uses.","What the data is used for, how long it is retained, who can access it, and whether the vendor trains on it.","How many students will use it on day one.","Whether the chatbot has a friendly name."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Purpose, retention, access, and vendor training are the privacy questions a consent clause must resolve.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_4_purpose_blind_collection","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_privacy_and_consent_with_minors","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_4_privacy_and_consent_with_minors_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"The vendor's default retains chat logs for 24 months and trains on them. Which consent clause is the most defensible revision?","options":["Keep the default because the vendor designed it.","Require parental opt-in, restrict use to a teacher struggling-student alert, bar vendor training, and delete logs at term end.","Let students opt out individually after the fact.","Approve the tool but ask the vendor verbally to be careful."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The strong clause overrides the unsafe default with specific, auditable limits tied to the evidence.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_4_purpose_blind_collection","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","cat531_supplement_privacy_and_consent_with_minors"]}]}}